\page org_mitk_views_imagestatistics The Image Statistics View

\imageMacro{bar-chart2-dox.svg,"Icon of the Image Statistics View",2.00}

\section QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualSummary Summary

This view provides an easy interface to quickly compute some features of a whole image or a region of interest.

This document will tell you how to use this view, but it is assumed that you already know how to use MITK in general.

Please see \ref QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualDetails for more detailed information on usage and supported filters.

\section QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualDetails Details

Manual sections:

- \ref QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualOverview
- \ref QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualUsage

\section QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualOverview Overview

This view provides an easy interface to quickly compute some features of a whole image or a region of interest.

\imageMacro{QmitkMeasurementToolbox_Interface.png,"The interface",9.10}

\section QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualUsage Usage


To select the input data, either use the "Select data..." button at the top of the "Statistics" tab, or pick images and ROIs separately on the "Selected data" tab. Multiple images can be selected as long as they share the same geometry.

\imageMacro{QmitkImageStatistics_InputData.png,"Select the input data via the \"Selected data\" tab (marked red) or via the quick selection button (marked yellow)",10}

\subsection QmitkImageStatisticStatistics Statistics
After selection of an image, the image statistics are calculated and displayed in the "Statistics" section. If ROIs (segmentations or planar figures) are selected, the statistics are restricted to these ROIs and listed per ROI and, for segmentations, per label. For time data the statistics for each time step are calculated correspondingly.

An example of parts of the calculated statistical features (Mean, Median, Std. dev., RMS, Max, Max position, Min, Min position, Voxels, Volume [mm³], Skewness, Kurtosis, Uniformity, Entropy, MPP, UPP and Variance) for different timesteps and different masks is pictured below.

Hover over a column header to see what the column stands for. The values of a column share a fixed number of decimal places, chosen so that the largest value of the column is shown with a useful precision. The "Copy to clipboard" button exports the unrounded values.

\imageMacro{QmitkImageStatistics_statistics.png, "Example of parts of the statistics for different masks and timestamps", 1.00}

Check "Ignore zero-valued voxels" to hide voxels with grayvalue zero.

\subsection QmitkImageStatisticHistogram Histogram

Beneath the statistics window is the histogram window, which shows the histogram of the current selection.

The histogram is only available for a single image with no or one ROI. When multiple images or multiple ROIs are selected, the histogram section is hidden.

For a segmentation with several labels, each label has its own histogram, drawn in the color of the label. Initially only the histogram of the first label is shown. Use the checkboxes in front of the labels in the "Statistics" table to choose which histograms are shown. Where histograms overlap, they are drawn translucently, and the tooltip names the label of the hovered bar.

Draw a rectangle with the left mouse button to zoom in on the histogram. A right click zooms out one step, Ctrl and right click zooms out to the full range, and the mouse wheel zooms the gray value range in and out around the cursor. Pan the view by dragging with the middle mouse button. The visible gray value range is also shown in the "Zoom range" fields below the plot, where you can type an exact range or reset the zoom with the "Reset" button. The zoom is kept when the shown labels, the time step or the number of bins change. The number of histogram bins can be changed after unchecking "Use default".

If the ROI is an open planar figure, such as a line or a path, an intensity profile along the figure is shown instead of the histogram.

A tooltip is available by hovering over one of the bins.

\subsection QmitkImageStatisticsUserManualCopytoClipboard Copy to Clipboard

The "Statistics" and "Histogram" sections each have a "Copy to clipboard" button, allowing to copy the respective data in tabular text format to the clipboard.
Be aware of the following information regarding this functionality:
\li The values are separated by tabulator.
\li  Numeric values will be converted using the system language (e.g. with German as system language the decimal separator will be ",", with English it will be '.').
\li  One can directly copy'n'paste into Excel (and alike) as long as Excel supports/is configured to the system language

*/
